1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,190
Now that we've built
the Linux kernel,

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:04,395
we're going to talk
about the last step

3
00:00:04,395 --> 00:00:07,230
in our embedded Linux
system build process,

4
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:09,795
which is to build our
root file system.

5
00:00:09,795 --> 00:00:12,030
Learning objectives. We're
going to learn about

6
00:00:12,030 --> 00:00:14,115
what's in a minimal
root filesystem.

7
00:00:14,115 --> 00:00:16,500
The file system
hierarchy standard that

8
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:18,990
dictates the contents
of a root filesystem.

9
00:00:18,990 --> 00:00:22,080
Then we're going to
talk about busy box,

10
00:00:22,080 --> 00:00:23,670
which is going to be useful for

11
00:00:23,670 --> 00:00:26,445
us in generating our
minimal root filesystem.

12
00:00:26,445 --> 00:00:29,240
If you stop at the end of

13
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,510
the last video and you
try to start QEMU and use

14
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:34,655
a command like the
one that we specified

15
00:00:34,655 --> 00:00:37,430
earlier that we showed

16
00:00:37,430 --> 00:00:40,190
earlier that we're going
to use for Assignment 3,

17
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:42,830
what you'll note is something
like this where you see

18
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:45,485
a kernel panic unable
to mount root fs.

19
00:00:45,485 --> 00:00:48,560
What's missing is because we
need our root filesystem.

20
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:50,570
We've built the kernel,
we built the device tree,

21
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:52,430
we've built modules, but we

22
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:55,225
haven't actually built
the root filesystem.

23
00:00:55,225 --> 00:00:59,485
You have a few different
options for root filesystem.

24
00:00:59,485 --> 00:01:01,520
One option is initramfs,

25
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:02,870
and this is really just a set of

26
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:05,165
files that gets
extracted into RAM.

27
00:01:05,165 --> 00:01:07,100
The typical use for this is to

28
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,150
set up your real
root filesystem.

29
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:10,760
In other words, lot of

30
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,055
times you might end up
with a scenario where

31
00:01:13,055 --> 00:01:16,160
your real root filesystem
is located on some type of

32
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:17,870
hardware that needs
additional drivers

33
00:01:17,870 --> 00:01:19,985
that aren't built
into your kernel.

34
00:01:19,985 --> 00:01:23,265
Or there might be other
reasons you have to

35
00:01:23,265 --> 00:01:26,120
prepare the storage that's

36
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:28,550
really holding your
root filesystem.

37
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:30,290
Anytime you have a
scenario like that,

38
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:34,640
you can use initramfs
as a way to hold a copy

39
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:36,860
of the root filesystem
in RAM that allows

40
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:39,605
you to get further on
in the boot process.

41
00:01:39,605 --> 00:01:42,035
In our case, we're just going
to use it for simplicity.

42
00:01:42,035 --> 00:01:43,310
We're going to use it instead of

43
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:45,095
having to try to
create a disk image,

44
00:01:45,095 --> 00:01:46,850
just takes one step
out of the process

45
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:50,890
to use initramfs instead.

46
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:53,990
The initramfs is just
going to be extracted

47
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:57,095
into RAM and that's going
to be our filesystem.

48
00:01:57,095 --> 00:02:01,280
Another option here that
you would typically use,

49
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:03,350
possibly more likely use

50
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:04,760
depending on whether
or not you needed

51
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:08,060
the initramfs would be just
specifying a block device.

52
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:10,970
If you had an image stored
on disk and you can

53
00:02:10,970 --> 00:02:13,760
just point it to you that
image as a block device,

54
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,590
then that wouldn't be
a second way you can

55
00:02:15,590 --> 00:02:17,510
use the root equals
parameter to point

56
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:21,170
to the device you want to move.

57
00:02:21,170 --> 00:02:22,670
The contents. What's
going to go into

58
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:25,440
our minimal root filesystem?

59
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:31,445
Init, content is going to
be what starts the system.

60
00:02:31,445 --> 00:02:33,740
Usually these are going
to be startup scripts.

61
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:37,160
Then the shell is going
to be what handles

62
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,280
shell scripts and runs
our command prompt,

63
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,640
our interactive command prompt.

64
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,130
Then daemons that we mentioned

65
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,810
that are the
background programs,

66
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:47,600
they kicked off that
provides services.

67
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:49,340
Other things that
are going to be in

68
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:50,900
our minimal root filesystem,

69
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:52,790
we'll have shared libraries.

70
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:54,845
Our applications are
going to be using,

71
00:02:54,845 --> 00:02:56,885
we'll have configuration
files which you

72
00:02:56,885 --> 00:02:59,010
usually live in etc sys.

73
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:00,620
We'll have dev nodes under dev

74
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:02,615
that we use to interact
with the kernel.

75
00:03:02,615 --> 00:03:05,990
Then we'll have proc and
sys pseudo file systems,

76
00:03:05,990 --> 00:03:09,490
again where we can do
some kernel interaction.

77
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:12,334
Then finally you'll also
have kernel modules,

78
00:03:12,334 --> 00:03:15,215
which would live
under /lib/modules,

79
00:03:15,215 --> 00:03:17,000
and then based on
the kernel version.

80
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,160
The reason for this is
because kernel modules

81
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,300
are going to be compiled
for a specific version.

82
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:22,400
If you want to support

83
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,950
multiple kernel versions
with your root filesystem,

84
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:26,000
you'd have different directories

85
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,470
under root modules
for each kernel.

86
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:32,675
Where does the kernel expect

87
00:03:32,675 --> 00:03:35,300
all these files to be located
in the root filesystem?

88
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:38,045
This is again, another
trick question,

89
00:03:38,045 --> 00:03:40,730
because it turns out the
kernel really doesn't have

90
00:03:40,730 --> 00:03:42,110
any expectations about

91
00:03:42,110 --> 00:03:43,760
the content and the
root filesystem.

92
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:45,950
This was a design goal to keep

93
00:03:45,950 --> 00:03:48,980
the kernel and the root file
system completely decoupled,

94
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:50,800
or decoupled as
much as possible.

95
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,820
Really the only thing
that the kernel

96
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:55,130
expects about the root
filesystem is that it

97
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:59,280
can call some application
to initialize the system.

98
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,610
That's what's specified on
that kernel command line

99
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:03,980
that we showed for
QEMU earlier where we

100
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:09,440
specified slash and slash
SH as they enter program.

101
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:10,675
What about programs?

102
00:04:10,675 --> 00:04:12,170
What expectations do programs

103
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:14,990
have about the content
of the root filesystem?

104
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:17,495
There there're definitely
are expectations

105
00:04:17,495 --> 00:04:19,490
that userspace programs have

106
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:21,230
about how other
userspace programs,

107
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:22,280
for instance, will use

108
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:23,720
the root filesystem or

109
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:25,655
how you will configure
their root filesystem.

110
00:04:25,655 --> 00:04:27,635
Those expectations are
defining what's called the

111
00:04:27,635 --> 00:04:31,415
filesystem hierarchy
standard or FHS.

112
00:04:31,415 --> 00:04:34,715
The content of the file
system hierarchy standard

113
00:04:34,715 --> 00:04:36,230
defines what all the

114
00:04:36,230 --> 00:04:38,795
different directories
in the root filesystem,

115
00:04:38,795 --> 00:04:40,280
how they should be used.

116
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:45,485
/bin is the location for
programs for all users,

117
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:47,980
including programs
that are used at boot.

118
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:50,850
/dev would be for a device
nodes and other files.

119
00:04:50,850 --> 00:04:52,175
This is where we're
going to create

120
00:04:52,175 --> 00:04:53,660
nodes that will let
us interact with

121
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:55,010
devices in the Linux kernel

122
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:57,520
and connect to specific modules.

123
00:04:57,520 --> 00:04:59,480
/etc is where all the system

124
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:01,295
configuration files
are going to live.

125
00:05:01,295 --> 00:05:03,215
/lib are the shared libraries.

126
00:05:03,215 --> 00:05:05,915
Proc and sys are the
sysfs file systems,

127
00:05:05,915 --> 00:05:08,195
again, allow us to
interact with the kernel.

128
00:05:08,195 --> 00:05:11,060
Then sbin is where programs for

129
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:12,920
the system administrator
that are used as

130
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:15,335
boot again are going to live.

131
00:05:15,335 --> 00:05:19,020
If you see something in
/sbin and not in /bin,

132
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:22,175
it's because the /sbin one

133
00:05:22,175 --> 00:05:24,260
might be restricted
in terms of use and

134
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:27,210
expected to only be used
by the root account.

135
00:05:27,690 --> 00:05:30,910
/tmp is where temporary
files live in,

136
00:05:30,910 --> 00:05:32,470
depending on how that's bounded,

137
00:05:32,470 --> 00:05:34,135
it might actually be a RAM disk,

138
00:05:34,135 --> 00:05:35,560
and so these files might be

139
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,810
automatically deleted
every time you boot.

140
00:05:37,810 --> 00:05:41,350
Then /usr, usr/bin,
and usr/sbin.

141
00:05:41,350 --> 00:05:42,640
These are additional programs,

142
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,170
libraries, and utilities.

143
00:05:44,170 --> 00:05:46,825
Probably in the case of
usr/bin and usr/sbin,

144
00:05:46,825 --> 00:05:50,200
these would be ones that
aren't used as a part of boot.

145
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:53,320
Then another directory specified

146
00:05:53,320 --> 00:05:55,735
in the file system
hierarchy standard is /var,

147
00:05:55,735 --> 00:05:58,300
and this is for files
modified at runtime.

148
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:01,255
One common location
here is /var/log,

149
00:06:01,255 --> 00:06:03,940
which is going to
contain our logs.

150
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:06,655
One difference between
temp and var is that

151
00:06:06,655 --> 00:06:09,595
var is typically going to
be retained after boot.

152
00:06:09,595 --> 00:06:11,350
There may be some
subdirectories under

153
00:06:11,350 --> 00:06:13,495
var that don't meet
this, but in general,

154
00:06:13,495 --> 00:06:17,470
tmp might be replaced
every boot in var that

155
00:06:17,470 --> 00:06:19,090
at least some
content probably is

156
00:06:19,090 --> 00:06:22,490
likely persistent across boots.

157
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:27,355
Now that we know what's
in a root file system,

158
00:06:27,355 --> 00:06:29,140
how would we want to create one?

159
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:32,780
This is part of what we'll
be doing with Assignment 3.

160
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:35,140
We need to start by creating

161
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:36,730
a folder tree where
we're going to put

162
00:06:36,730 --> 00:06:38,440
all the contents that
will ultimately be

163
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:41,050
located in our root file system.

164
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:44,035
That's really the list of
directories right here.

165
00:06:44,035 --> 00:06:46,000
It's as simple as using mkdir

166
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,480
and creating all this
tree of directories.

167
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:54,715
When all the mkdir
step get done,

168
00:06:54,715 --> 00:06:58,000
you should see a tree that
looks like this that would be

169
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,350
located in essentially your
skeleton root file system.

170
00:07:02,350 --> 00:07:06,160
You can create this
anywhere on the system.

171
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:08,170
In Assignment 3, we'll use

172
00:07:08,170 --> 00:07:10,615
a temp directory
for this purpose.

173
00:07:10,615 --> 00:07:14,110
Now we've got the root file
system structure setup

174
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:15,910
in terms of the directories,

175
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:17,185
but how about the files?

176
00:07:17,185 --> 00:07:19,630
How do we get the structure

177
00:07:19,630 --> 00:07:21,640
actually filled with the files

178
00:07:21,640 --> 00:07:23,680
that we need in the
root file system?

179
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:26,320
It turns out there's a
really great utility that we

180
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:29,005
can use for this,
that's called BusyBox.

181
00:07:29,005 --> 00:07:33,505
BusyBox, what it is is
essentially a single application.

182
00:07:33,505 --> 00:07:36,115
A single binary executable that

183
00:07:36,115 --> 00:07:39,730
implements a whole bunch of
essential Linux programs.

184
00:07:39,730 --> 00:07:42,354
Rather than having each
one of these programs

185
00:07:42,354 --> 00:07:45,040
individually compiled into
a separate executable,

186
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:48,295
BusyBox provides one
executable that does them all.

187
00:07:48,295 --> 00:07:52,210
In order to understand which
command it should run,

188
00:07:52,210 --> 00:07:54,130
it uses a symbolic link

189
00:07:54,130 --> 00:07:57,290
to the name of the command
that you're trying to run.

190
00:07:57,390 --> 00:08:00,820
For instance, if I want to use

191
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:04,270
the cat utility to
cut out a file,

192
00:08:04,270 --> 00:08:06,805
I would just have
a symbolic link

193
00:08:06,805 --> 00:08:09,460
from bin/cat to BusyBox,

194
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:11,980
and then when I run bin/cat,

195
00:08:11,980 --> 00:08:14,080
BusyBox will be
able to see how it

196
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:16,210
was invoked and know
that I've tried

197
00:08:16,210 --> 00:08:18,565
to run the cat
command and then run

198
00:08:18,565 --> 00:08:21,760
a version of the cat command
that's specific to BusyBox.

199
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:25,525
One thing to know about
BusyBox is that often

200
00:08:25,525 --> 00:08:28,600
not all the arguments
are supported

201
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:31,795
and not all the features for
every command are supported.

202
00:08:31,795 --> 00:08:33,610
But for our case where
we're just trying

203
00:08:33,610 --> 00:08:35,470
to bring up a really
simple system.

204
00:08:35,470 --> 00:08:38,230
It's a much easier option
than trying to pull

205
00:08:38,230 --> 00:08:39,250
the source for every one of

206
00:08:39,250 --> 00:08:42,290
these utilities and to
file them individually.

207
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:46,345
In order to make and
install BusyBox,

208
00:08:46,345 --> 00:08:48,220
we'll do steps very

209
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:50,425
similar to what we're doing
with the kernel build.

210
00:08:50,425 --> 00:08:53,005
We'll do a distclean,
a defconfig,

211
00:08:53,005 --> 00:08:54,640
and then we'll
specify our arch and

212
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:56,560
our cross-compile and do a make.

213
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:58,210
Finally, we'll specify

214
00:08:58,210 --> 00:09:00,280
the location of our
root directory,

215
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:01,705
where we want it to populate,

216
00:09:01,705 --> 00:09:07,135
and do this make install step.

217
00:09:07,135 --> 00:09:09,070
When we do the
make install step,

218
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:11,020
the install step is
going to not only

219
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:13,345
copy BusyBox into our
root file system,

220
00:09:13,345 --> 00:09:17,410
but create all those siblings
for us so that the commands

221
00:09:17,410 --> 00:09:19,195
that we need to run will be

222
00:09:19,195 --> 00:09:22,400
Simulink back to the
BusyBox executable.

223
00:09:23,130 --> 00:09:25,734
Next we need to,

224
00:09:25,734 --> 00:09:29,170
because we're running BusyBox on

225
00:09:29,170 --> 00:09:31,825
our root file system

226
00:09:31,825 --> 00:09:34,180
and BusyBox has
some dependencies

227
00:09:34,180 --> 00:09:36,250
in terms of libraries
that it needs,

228
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:38,815
we need to find out what
dependencies those are,

229
00:09:38,815 --> 00:09:40,615
and we need to add
those dependencies

230
00:09:40,615 --> 00:09:42,865
into our root file system.

231
00:09:42,865 --> 00:09:48,010
The readelf command is very
useful for this purpose.

232
00:09:48,010 --> 00:09:50,440
The readelf that we're going to

233
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:53,305
use is going to be based
on our cross-compiler.

234
00:09:53,305 --> 00:09:55,720
We'll prefix the
readelf command with

235
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:59,260
our cross-compile string
that we're using with the,

236
00:09:59,260 --> 00:10:01,235
for instance, the
compile commands.

237
00:10:01,235 --> 00:10:04,980
When we do that, we can look
for the program interpreter.

238
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:06,600
This is what's
going to ultimately

239
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:08,370
run the BusyBox executable.

240
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:09,945
We can see a reference to

241
00:10:09,945 --> 00:10:13,215
a file that's needed for
the program interpreter.

242
00:10:13,215 --> 00:10:15,785
We see that that's located
in the lib directory.

243
00:10:15,785 --> 00:10:20,350
We'll need to populate
this file from

244
00:10:20,350 --> 00:10:25,500
our Cicero and place it
into our lib directory.

245
00:10:25,500 --> 00:10:31,910
Similarly, if we search in
the output of readelf for

246
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:35,090
the BusyBox utility and search
for shared library we'll

247
00:10:35,090 --> 00:10:36,905
see a list of shared libraries

248
00:10:36,905 --> 00:10:38,855
that are needed for BusyBox.

249
00:10:38,855 --> 00:10:40,610
For each one of these files,

250
00:10:40,610 --> 00:10:42,320
we need to locate them in

251
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:45,500
our Cicero for our
cross-compile toolchain.

252
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:47,255
So wherever you installed
the tool chain,

253
00:10:47,255 --> 00:10:51,335
you need to locate those
files in the Cicero

254
00:10:51,335 --> 00:10:55,910
and then you need to copy them
into the library location.

255
00:10:55,910 --> 00:10:58,655
Now since we're using
an AR-64 target,

256
00:10:58,655 --> 00:11:00,560
rather than placing
in slash lib we'll

257
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,530
place them in slash lib64.

258
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:06,530
So that should take
care of most of

259
00:11:06,530 --> 00:11:07,610
the content that we're
going to need in

260
00:11:07,610 --> 00:11:09,215
the root file system.

261
00:11:09,215 --> 00:11:10,910
One other thing that
we're going to need to

262
00:11:10,910 --> 00:11:13,190
add, however, our devices,

263
00:11:13,190 --> 00:11:16,940
and there aren't many
devices we're going to need,

264
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:20,495
since our system is
a very simple one.

265
00:11:20,495 --> 00:11:21,230
But there are a couple of

266
00:11:21,230 --> 00:11:22,310
devices that we're
going to need.

267
00:11:22,310 --> 00:11:23,975
One is a null device.

268
00:11:23,975 --> 00:11:27,469
This is just a device
that provides zeros

269
00:11:27,469 --> 00:11:29,660
or essentially has a place

270
00:11:29,660 --> 00:11:32,105
to dump content that's unused,

271
00:11:32,105 --> 00:11:33,680
and some of our startup scripts

272
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:36,635
are going to expect
that to exist.

273
00:11:36,635 --> 00:11:38,435
So we'll want to create that.

274
00:11:38,435 --> 00:11:41,675
Then we'll also need
a console device that

275
00:11:41,675 --> 00:11:45,960
is available for interacting
through the terminal.

276
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:48,730
So for each one of
these, we'll use

277
00:11:48,730 --> 00:11:52,870
the make node command and
we'll specify the name,

278
00:11:52,870 --> 00:11:56,925
the type, and the major
and minor number.

279
00:11:56,925 --> 00:11:58,760
So looking for example,

280
00:11:58,760 --> 00:12:00,725
at the null device,

281
00:12:00,725 --> 00:12:04,505
we can do a make node -M 666.

282
00:12:04,505 --> 00:12:11,285
So this would be the access
permissions of the node,

283
00:12:11,285 --> 00:12:15,350
and then /dev/null is the
node that we're creating.

284
00:12:15,350 --> 00:12:18,200
C specifies that it's
the character type,

285
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:20,150
and then one and three are

286
00:12:20,150 --> 00:12:23,435
just known major and minor
numbers for our null device.

287
00:12:23,435 --> 00:12:26,210
So we need to run
this command to

288
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:29,210
create the dev null device
in our root file system.

289
00:12:29,210 --> 00:12:31,250
And similarly, we'll
need to create

290
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:33,830
a dev console device using

291
00:12:33,830 --> 00:12:37,460
swapping out the major
numbers 5 and minor number 1.

292
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:40,010
So now that we've got a
root file system created,

293
00:12:40,010 --> 00:12:42,515
how do we actually
use this with target?

294
00:12:42,515 --> 00:12:44,960
So there's a few
different options that

295
00:12:44,960 --> 00:12:46,820
are kind of standard ways

296
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:49,085
to use a root file
system with the target.

297
00:12:49,085 --> 00:12:50,570
One is a RAM disk,

298
00:12:50,570 --> 00:12:52,670
which is essentially a disk
image that's loaded in

299
00:12:52,670 --> 00:12:55,895
RAM by the bootloader
at boot time.

300
00:12:55,895 --> 00:12:57,830
Second is a disk image,

301
00:12:57,830 --> 00:13:00,230
so something that's
written to like an SD card

302
00:13:00,230 --> 00:13:03,645
or maybe an actual
spinning disk.

303
00:13:03,645 --> 00:13:07,000
Then, a third option is
network file system.

304
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,760
So it's one that we can just
access over the network.

305
00:13:09,760 --> 00:13:11,530
Again, in our simple scenario,

306
00:13:11,530 --> 00:13:13,900
we're going to use the
RAM disk and a disk

307
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:16,740
that's just loaded into
RAM by our bootloader.

308
00:13:16,740 --> 00:13:18,950
Our bootloader is
essentially going to

309
00:13:18,950 --> 00:13:22,205
be our qemu emulation
environment.

310
00:13:22,205 --> 00:13:24,980
So to prepare our
RAM disk will use

311
00:13:24,980 --> 00:13:28,670
the cpio utility to
create a.cpio file.

312
00:13:28,670 --> 00:13:31,340
This cpio utility is just
going to essentially

313
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:34,100
bundle the content of
our root file system in

314
00:13:34,100 --> 00:13:37,520
a format that's understood
by qemu that we can pass

315
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:42,110
to the qemu as a Run command.

316
00:13:42,110 --> 00:13:44,270
Qemu is going to know
how to extract that

317
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:47,345
into a RAM disk that
we can then boot into.

318
00:13:47,345 --> 00:13:51,350
So you can use a command
like this with cpio,

319
00:13:51,350 --> 00:13:53,660
this is just saying
find everything in

320
00:13:53,660 --> 00:13:55,760
my root file system that I've

321
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:58,670
set up as using the
previous instructions.

322
00:13:58,670 --> 00:14:03,605
Then create a cpio file
using an owner of root:root.

323
00:14:03,605 --> 00:14:05,555
Again, for our simple case,

324
00:14:05,555 --> 00:14:08,255
we're only going to have
a single root user.

325
00:14:08,255 --> 00:14:10,970
So you need to make sure
that you don't specify

326
00:14:10,970 --> 00:14:12,905
your user account on

327
00:14:12,905 --> 00:14:14,180
the root file system because

328
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:16,385
that user is not going to exist.

329
00:14:16,385 --> 00:14:18,500
You'll use a command
like this to

330
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:21,500
create the initramfs.cpio file.

331
00:14:21,500 --> 00:14:24,710
Then you'll compress
it with gzip to match

332
00:14:24,710 --> 00:14:28,025
the format that is expected
for our qemu start command.

333
00:14:28,025 --> 00:14:31,520
To compress, you just
use this gzip command.

334
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:34,535
So now we should have all
the pieces put together.

335
00:14:34,535 --> 00:14:36,410
We've got the kernel,

336
00:14:36,410 --> 00:14:37,880
we've got the root file system,

337
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:40,670
and we've got the content

338
00:14:40,670 --> 00:14:43,175
that we need populated
into the file system,

339
00:14:43,175 --> 00:14:45,950
and we've got that
all into a cpio file

340
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:49,175
and compressed into a gz file.

341
00:14:49,175 --> 00:14:53,255
Now we're ready to
actually run in qemu.

342
00:14:53,255 --> 00:14:56,390
So there is a star
qemu terminal script

343
00:14:56,390 --> 00:14:59,000
that's gonna be provided with
your assignment resource,

344
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,595
and that it will have
a command line that

345
00:15:01,595 --> 00:15:05,495
will take all the
components to be built and

346
00:15:05,495 --> 00:15:08,405
issue a qemu command
that's going to start

347
00:15:08,405 --> 00:15:13,610
our emulator using the content

348
00:15:13,610 --> 00:15:15,530
that we built as a part of this

349
00:15:15,530 --> 00:15:18,605
in previous lectures source.

350
00:15:18,605 --> 00:15:21,950
You should now be ready
to move on to Part 2

351
00:15:21,950 --> 00:15:24,695
of Assignment 3 and actually

352
00:15:24,695 --> 00:15:28,040
build and boot your own custom

353
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:31,560
embedded Linux distribution.